<GuideLayout
  title="How to Test a Phone Screen — OLED Burn-in, Tint and Dead Pixels"
  description="Test a phone screen in the browser rather than with a video: compression hides real faults and invents fake ones. Covers the iOS Safari full-screen limitation, auto-brightness, OLED burn-in, PWM flicker and low-brightness tint."
  heading="How to test a phone screen"
  lede="A phone panel fails differently from a monitor, and the way most people test one — playing a dead pixel video — is actively worse than useless. Here is what works and what a browser genuinely cannot do."
  updated="2026-08-27"
  slug="how-to-test-a-phone-screen"
>
  <Card surface="soft" elevation="e1" padding="lg">
    <p class="text-body-sm font-medium text-ink">In short</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-body">
      Use flat CSS colour fills, never a video &mdash; compression both hides
      real single-pixel faults and manufactures fake ones. Turn off
      auto-brightness and True Tone first. On iPhone, add this site to the Home
      Screen to get a genuinely full-screen fill. Then run the
      <a href="/test/sequence">guided test</a>, and check
      <a href="/test/grey-50">mid grey</a> for OLED burn-in.
    </p>
  </Card>

  <h2>Why a video test is worse than no test</h2>
  <p>
    Dead pixel test videos are the default advice and they are wrong in both
    directions. Lossy video compression divides the frame into blocks and throws
    away detail inside each one, so a single faulty pixel gets averaged into its
    neighbours and disappears &mdash; the exact fault you are looking for is the
    first thing the codec discards. At the same time, compression <em>adds</em>
    artefacts: blocking on flat fields and mosquito noise shimmering around
    high-contrast edges, both of which look like panel faults and are not.
  </p>
  <p>
    A browser painting a flat CSS colour has no codec in the path. The value
    reaches the panel exactly as specified, every pixel identical, so anything you
    see is the screen. That is the whole reason this site is built the way it is.
  </p>

  <h2>The iPhone Safari limitation, stated plainly</h2>
  <p>
    iOS Safari only exposes the Fullscreen API for <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>
    elements. A colour fill on a web page therefore <strong>cannot</strong> truly
    cover the screen in Safari on iPhone, and any site claiming otherwise is
    describing something else. Three workarounds, in order of how much they
    actually help:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Add to Home Screen &mdash; this genuinely works.</strong> Launched
      from the Home Screen the site opens in standalone mode with no browser
      chrome at all, so the fill covers everything but the status bar area. This
      is the only option that gets you close to a real full-screen fill.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scroll down so the address bar collapses.</strong> Helps, but only
      gives you more of a fill, not all of it &mdash; the toolbar shrinks rather
      than vanishing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rotate to landscape.</strong> Safari hides more chrome in landscape
      on smaller iPhones, so it is worth doing, but again this is more fill rather
      than a full one.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On Android, Chrome grants the Fullscreen API to any element, so the fill
    covers the screen properly with no workaround needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Switch off everything adaptive first</h2>
  <p>
    Auto-brightness, True Tone, Night Shift and any adaptive colour or reading
    mode all have to be off before you judge colour or uniformity. These features
    actively shift white point and luminance <em>while you are looking</em>, which
    means a tint you think you have found may be the phone reacting to your room,
    and a uniformity difference between two halves of a slow pan may be the
    backlight ramping. Set brightness manually, somewhere in the middle.
  </p>

  <h2>OLED faults, which are the ones you will actually find</h2>
  <p>
    Most modern phones are OLED, and OLED fails differently from LCD. Classic
    stuck and dead pixels are rare on a new OLED panel; these are what turn up
    instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Burn-in and image retention.</strong> Load
    <a href="/test/grey-50">mid grey</a> and look for a ghost of the status bar
    across the top, or of the keyboard across the lower third. A faint ghost that
    fades within a minute or two is temporary image retention and is normal
    behaviour, not a defect. A ghost that is still there after the screen has been
    showing other content for a while is permanent burn-in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>PWM flicker.</strong> Many OLED phones dim by pulse-width modulation
    &mdash; switching the panel on and off rapidly rather than reducing its
    output &mdash; and some people get headaches or eye strain from it at low
    brightness. A browser cannot measure this: the flicker happens in the
    panel&rsquo;s driver, below anything JavaScript can observe. The standard
    detection trick is to point a second phone&rsquo;s camera at the screen in
    slow-motion mode at low brightness and look for rolling dark bands. Be clear
    about what that is: a yes-or-no indication that PWM is present, not a
    measurement of its frequency or depth.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pink or green tint at low brightness.</strong> Drop brightness to its
    minimum in a dark room and look at a dark grey field. Many OLED panels shift
    towards pink or green at the bottom of their range, and pronounced,
    unevenly-distributed tint is a known panel fault on some production runs
    &mdash; worth photographing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>No backlight bleed, as a category.</strong> Each OLED pixel emits its
    own light, so there is no backlight to leak. If you are looking for bleed on
    an OLED phone you are looking for something that cannot exist. Black
    uniformity is still worth checking, but you are checking for tint and for dead
    emitters, not for edge leakage.
  </p>

  <h2>Which tests transfer to a phone</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fully:</strong> the colour fills and the
    <a href="/test/sequence">guided run</a>, plus
    <a href="/test/uniformity">uniformity</a> and the grey fields for burn-in.
    These are exactly as valid on a handset as on a desktop panel.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Partly:</strong> <a href="/test/refresh-rate">refresh rate</a>. It
    will confirm the rate the browser is receiving, which is useful on a variable
    refresh rate phone, but a phone drops its rate aggressively to save power, so
    a low reading may be correct behaviour rather than a fault.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Not really:</strong> viewing angle and response time. You hold a phone
    and tilt it constantly, so off-axis performance is something you already know
    about, and judging ghosting on a small handheld display you are moving
    yourself tells you very little.
  </p>

  <h2>Cracked screen versus panel fault</h2>
  <p>
    If the symptoms started after a drop, this is physical damage rather than a
    warranty defect, and describing it as a pixel fault will not help your case. A
    black blotch that spreads over hours or days, coloured vertical or horizontal
    lines, or a bright bloom radiating from one point are all impact damage to the
    panel or its ribbon connector &mdash; even when the front glass is unbroken.
    That is a repair or an insurance claim, not a pixel policy question.
  </p>
